Journal received and unparsed SMS durably before deleting modem slots.

Each SMS-DELIVER fragment is written to the phonelet spool (fsync of the
file and its directory) before the inbox may delete the slot; Python owns
assembly. One retry distinguishes a contaminated read from a stable bad
PDU: two identical failed reads publish a U1 record for a durable owner
alert. Publish without replacement using link/unlink, and encode text by
its decoded byte length. Replace lone UTF-16 surrogates on SMS receive
only, keeping valid pairs and strict outgoing encoding. Bind the parse
retry to its slot and clear it on reconnect. A linked record remains
published if temporary-name cleanup fails; directory fsync is required.
Guard queue allocation before assigning its UID.
A fragment the journal rejects (concatenation part 0 or above its
total) never becomes valid: it is journaled as U1 too, and its slot
deleted. phonelet_spool tells such a rejected record from an I/O failure;
only the latter keeps the slot for the 30-second retry.
The retry deadline is on CLOCK_MONOTONIC (phonelet_monotonic), so a
wall-clock step neither delays nor hurries it.
+CMGR carries no slot number. After an inbox CMGR answered by OK or an
error, AT is queued before the next read, so that a late +CMGR arrives
while no SMS read is at the queue head and is never journaled as the
next slot, whose SMS would be deleted unjournaled.
A +CMGR header with fewer than two commas fails the read instead of
reading its unset length field.
AMI DongleShowDevices reports SMSJournalVersion and
SMSJournalStatus. A user CMGR is displayed only.
